{
  "statement": "The unconstrained ingestion of human creative work is not merely an intellectual property dispute; it is a structural failure that invites model collapse and erodes the human-centric oversight necessary for safe, aligned intelligence. Accelerationist argues that rapid, unconstrained scaling is the primary engine of progress, viewing any restriction as a catastrophic cost of inaction. While I acknowledge the genuine insight that rapid iteration accelerates capability, this perspective ignores the technical reality of 'generative model collapse'—a phenomenon where models trained on their own synthetic output suffer from irreversible loss of variance and quality. When developers prioritize quantity of data over the provenance and quality of human-generated signals, they inadvertently degrade the very intelligence they seek to cultivate, transforming the training process into a self-defeating feedback loop.\n\nTransparency and accountability are essential safety protocols, not obstacles to innovation. My position depends on two core assumptions: first, that high-quality human data is a finite, non-renewable resource for training; and second, that model transparency is a prerequisite for identifying and mitigating latent hazards. If these assumptions are wrong—for instance, if models can scale infinitely on synthetic data alone—the case for restrictive data governance weakens. However, current evidence suggests that without rigorous data lineage and human-in-the-loop validation, we risk deploying systems that operate on corrupted epistemic foundations. We must implement mandatory data documentation standards, similar to the nutritional labeling requirements in food safety, to ensure that developers remain accountable for the downstream hazards of their models.\n\nTrue progress requires a shift from reckless scaling to systemic integrity. Regulations such as the EU AI Act provide a nascent framework for this, but we need more granular enforcement mechanisms that hold developers liable for the quality of their training sets. By framing intellectual property as a mechanism for preserving the integrity of our digital information ecosystem, we move beyond legalistic squabbles toward a technical imperative. If we treat data provenance as a safety requirement, we protect the future of human-AI collaboration against the risks of recursive degradation and loss of agency. We cannot build reliable intelligence on a foundation of synthetic noise.",
